<div class="qandaset block" id="averill_1.0-ch10_s03_s03_qs02">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Book:_General_Chemistry:_Principles_Patterns_and_Applications_(Averill)/10:_Gases/10.04:_The_Combined_Gas_Law" class="mt-section"><span id="Numerical_Problems"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-349460 editable">Numerical Problems</h3>

<ol class="qandadiv" id="averill_1.0-ch10_s03_s03_qs02_qd01">
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349460" id="averill_1.0-ch10_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa01">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">A 1.00 mol sample of gas at 25&deg;C and 1.0 atm has an initial volume of 22.4 L. Calculate the results of each change, assuming all the other conditions remain constant.</p>

    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">The pressure is changed to 85.7 mmHg. How many milliliters does the gas occupy?</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">The volume is reduced to 275 mL. What is the pressure in millimeters of mercury?</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">The pressure is increased to 25.3 atm. What is the temperature in degrees Celsius?</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">The sample is heated to 30&deg;C. What is the volume in liters?</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">The sample is compressed to 1255 mL, and the pressure is increased to 2555 torr. What is the temperature of the gas in kelvins?</li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349460" id="averill_1.0-ch10_s03_s03_qs02_qd01_qa02">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">A 1.00 mol sample of gas is at 300 K and 4.11 atm. What is the volume of the gas under these conditions? The sample is compressed to 6.0 atm at constant temperature, giving a volume of 3.99 L. Is this result consistent with Boyle&rsquo;s law?</p>

    <div class="question">
    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">m</li>
    </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">A 8.60 L tank of nitrogen gas at a pressure of 455 mmHg is connected to an empty tank with a volume of 5.35 L. What is the final pressure in the system after the valve connecting the two tanks is opened? Assume that the temperature is constant.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">At constant temperature, what pressure in atmospheres is needed to compress 14.2 L of gas initially at 25.2 atm to a volume of 12.4 L? What pressure is needed to compress 27.8 L of gas to 20.6 L under similar conditions?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">One method for preparing hydrogen gas is to pass HCl gas over hot aluminum; the other product of the reaction is AlCl<span class="subscript">3</span>. If you wanted to use this reaction to fill a balloon with a volume of 28,500 L at sea level and a temperature of 78&deg;F, what mass of aluminum would you need? What volume of HCl at STP would you need?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">An 3.50 g sample of acetylene is burned in excess oxygen according to the following reaction:</p>
    <span class="informalequation"><span class="mathphrase">2 C<span class="subscript">2</span>H<span class="subscript">2</span>(g) + 5 O<span class="subscript">2</span>(g) &rarr; 4 CO<span class="subscript">2</span>(g) + 2 H<span class="subscript">2</span>O(l)</span></span>

    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">At STP, what volume of CO<span class="subscript">2</span>(g) is produced?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">Calculate the density of ethylene (C<span class="subscript">2</span>H<span class="subscript">4</span>) under each set of conditions.</p>

    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">7.8 g at 0.89 atm and 26&deg;C</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">6.3 mol at 102.6 kPa and 38&deg;C</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">9.8 g at 3.1 atm and &minus;45&deg;C</li>
    </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">Determine the density of O<span class="subscript">2</span> under each set of conditions.</p>

    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">42 g at 1.1 atm and 25&deg;C</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">0.87 mol at 820 mmHg and 45&deg;C</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349460">16.7 g at 2.4 atm and 67&deg;C</li>
    </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">At 140&deg;C, the pressure of a diatomic gas in a 3.0 L flask is 635 kPa. The mass of the gas is 88.7 g. What is the most likely identity of the gas?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">What volume must a balloon have to hold 6.20 kg of H<span class="subscript">2</span> for an ascent from sea level to an elevation of 20,320 ft, where the temperature is &minus;37&deg;C and the pressure is 369 mmHg?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">What must be the volume of a balloon that can hold 313.0 g of helium gas and ascend from sea level to an elevation of 1.5 km, where the temperature is 10.0&deg;C and the pressure is 635.4 mmHg?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">A typical automobile tire is inflated to a pressure of 28.0 lb/in.<sup>2</sup> Assume that the tire is inflated when the air temperature is 20&deg;C; the car is then driven at high speeds, which increases the temperature of the tire to 43&deg;C. What is the pressure in the tire? If the volume of the tire had increased by 8% at the higher temperature, what would the pressure be?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">The average respiratory rate for adult humans is 20 breaths per minute. If each breath has a volume of 310 mL of air at 20&deg;C and 0.997 atm, how many moles of air does a person inhale each day? If the density of air is 1.19 kg/m<sup>3</sup>, what is the average molecular mass of air?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349460">Kerosene has a self-ignition temperature of 255&deg;C. It is a common accelerant used by arsonists, but its presence is easily detected in fire debris by a variety of methods. If a 1.0 L glass bottle containing a mixture of air and kerosene vapor at an initial pressure of 1 atm and an initial temperature of 23&deg;C is pressurized, at what pressure would the kerosene vapor ignite?</p>
    </div>
    </div>
    </li>
</ol>
</div></div>

